// ═══ PRE-JAVA 5: BROKEN ═══
// volatile: prevented caching but NOT reordering!
// Double-checked locking BROKEN:
// private static volatile Singleton instance;
// if (instance == null) { synchronized { if (instance == null) instance = new Singleton(); } }
// CPU could reorder: publish reference BEFORE constructor finishes!
// Another thread sees non-null instance with UNINITIALIZED fields!
// final fields: NO guarantee of visibility after constructor!
// class Foo { final int x = 42; } → another thread could see x = 0!

// ═══ JAVA 5 (JSR-133): FIXED! ═══
// volatile: FULL happens-before — no reordering across volatile read/write!
// Double-checked locking: WORKS with volatile! (volatile prevents reordering)
// final fields: FREEZE at end of constructor! Visible to all threads!
// Happens-before rules: formalized (sync, volatile, start, join, transitivity)
// java.util.concurrent: Executors, Locks, Atomic, ConcurrentHashMap, etc.

// ═══ JAVA 9: VarHandle ═══
// Fine-grained memory access modes (replaces sun.misc.Unsafe):
VarHandle vh = MethodHandles.lookup()
    .findVarHandle(MyClass.class, "counter", int.class);

vh.get(obj);                    // plain read (no ordering)
vh.getOpaque(obj);              // opaque: no tearing, but no ordering
vh.getAcquire(obj);             // acquire: sees all writes before matching release
vh.getVolatile(obj);            // full volatile semantics (strongest)

vh.set(obj, 42);                // plain write
vh.setRelease(obj, 42);        // release: all prior writes visible to acquire reader
vh.setVolatile(obj, 42);       // full volatile write

vh.compareAndSet(obj, 0, 1);   // CAS (volatile semantics)
vh.weakCompareAndSet(obj, 0, 1); // weak CAS (may fail spuriously)

// WHY VarHandle? Performance! Full volatile is expensive on some CPUs.
// acquire/release is CHEAPER than volatile on ARM/POWER architectures.
// Most developers: stick with volatile/synchronized. VarHandle: for framework authors.

// ═══ JAVA 21 ═══
// Virtual threads: lightweight (1KB stack), millions possible, JVM-managed
// Blocking IO on virtual thread: carrier thread freed automatically!
// Biased locking REMOVED: was optimization for single-thread lock (no CAS needed)
//   Removed because: complexity, maintenance cost, modern hardware doesn't benefit much
//   -XX:-UseBiasedLocking (was default, now removed entirely)
